Introduction
	This help section explains how XFaceMaker can be extended
	with new functions and widget classes.
	
	The principle is that a new instance of XFaceMaker can
	be generated by compiling a very simple main file
	with the XFaceMaker library libFm_e.a. C functions
	are provided to register new functions to the FACE
	interpreter and new classes that can be used like
	standard Motif classes.
	
Generating a New XFaceMaker
	The simplest way of creating a new version of XFaceMaker
	is to use the command `File/Save Appli Files/New Xfm'
	in XFaceMaker's main menu.
	
	This command prompts you for the base name of the new
	XFaceMaker, and generates a main C file and the Makefile
	to compile it. If you entered the base name `test',
	you will obtain a main file called `testXfmMain.c' and
	a Makefile called `testXfmMake'.
	
	You can compile the new XFaceMaker immediately with
	the command `File/Design Process/Build New Xfm'.
	
	Then, you can launch the new program without exiting
	from XFaceMaker: the command
	`File/Design Process/Launch New Xfm' will connect
	the new program in place of the current Design Process
	(see Help on Dual-Process Architecture). Of course,
	if you have not added any functions or widget classes,
	your new XFaceMaker does not provide more than the
	standard one. See `Adding Functions' and
	`Adding Widget Classes'.
	
Adding Functions
	You can register new functions to the FACE interpreter
	by calling the application function `FmAttachFunction'.
	
	For example, if you want to add the function `void FooBar(int)'
	so that it is predefined in the FACE interpreter, you
	must add the following line in the file testXfmMain.c:
	
	FmAttachFunction("FooBar", (FaceFunctionPtr)FooBar, "None", 1, "Int");
	
	You will also have to modify the Makefile to add the library
	or object file containing the function definition.
	
	Then, you can compile the new XFaceMaker and call the
	command `File/Design Process/Launch New Xfm' to launch it
	and connect it as a new Design Process.
	
Adding Widget Classes
	You can register new widget classes to XFaceMaker, for
	example third-party widget classes or classes you have
	developed yourself. Once registered, these classes
	can be used in XFaceMaker exactly like standard Motif
	classes.
	
	To register a new widget class, you will have to call
	the C function `FmAddWidgetClass'. If the class defines
	non-standard resources (i.e. resources which have a
	representation type unknown to Motif or Xt), you may
	have to call also the functions FmAddResourceType
	and FmAddRepresentation.
	
	See the documentation for more details on these
	functions.
	
